# Modern Bb horn, valves open (the Bb side of a double horn, no valve pressed), one held note: mouthpiece, bore from the mouthpiece to the bell rim, lip, microphone.
# render.sh prepends the schema and audio records and appends the per-note lip exponent and the schedule record, so one description serves every open-valve note.
#
# LABELS. Every number carries one: [M] measured in the cited source, [R] read from an image by this lane with the calibration stated, [B] borrowed from another instrument or another player, [N] a maker's nominal figure.
# UNKNOWN marks what no source this lane could open gives. Nothing is fitted to a recording. The pitch of a note is never an input: it is the lock the loop finds between the lip and the bore.
#
# --- what this card is ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The air column of the Bb side of a modern double horn by Engelbert Schmid (Germany, rotary valves, screw-on bell), valves open, measured by acoustic pulse reflectometry: Kemp, Chick, Campbell and Hendrie,
# 'Acoustic pulse reflectometry for the measurement of horn crooks', Acoustics'08 Paris, Figs 8 and 9, curve 'Bb Schmid' (with bell). The recorded horn (University of Iowa MIS, J. Wiskus) is of a model the Iowa pages do not state:
# this bore is a population stand-in for it, not that instrument, and the open Bb series is the only series it can play (the valve loops are not measured, so no valved note is claimed).
#
# --- bore: three parts, joined end to end ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. MOUTHPIECE, 2 cones: the Paxman '3A' formerly used with the Alexander double horn of the Edinburgh University Collection (MIMEd record 16358, as read on 2026-11-09; the record is behind an access check today):
#    cup diameter 16.6 mm [M], throat 4.5 mm [M], cup depth about 26 mm [M], shank depth 31.7 mm [M]. No cup or backbore contour is printed: the cup is ONE cone from the rim to the throat and the backbore ONE cone from the throat to the
#    receiver bore [B: straight cones between the printed ends]. ANOTHER HORN'S MOUTHPIECE, not the Schmid's own, and not the Iowa player's. The mouthpiece is taken to end where the reflectometer's profile starts (x = 0 below): the profile's first
#    diameter, 7.7 mm, is the leadpipe just beyond a shank tip (shank outer diameter 7.5 mm at its tip, the same record), not the receiver socket 9 mm wide; the alternative reading (shank inside the profile) would shorten the tube by 31.7 mm.
# 2. MAIN BORE, x = 0 to 2770 mm from the reflectometer's reference plane, 147 cones through stations every 20 mm [R]: the diameter of the 'Bb Schmid' curve read from the two raster figures of the paper
#    (560 x 420 px JPEGs): Fig 9 for x up to 1860 mm (diameter 7.7 to 15.8 mm; 0.026 mm and 4.6 mm per pixel), Fig 8 for 1880 to 2770 mm (diameter 15.8 to 98.8 mm; 0.272 mm and 8.4 mm per pixel). Calibration by least squares on
#    the plot-box ticks (residuals 0.0 to 0.44 px), curves isolated by colour mask; the reading error of one pixel is 0.03 mm in the main bore, 0.3 to 0.7 mm above 16 mm, 1 to 4 mm above 40 mm diameter; the source's own
#    bound is +-0.1 mm up to 1 m and +-0.8 mm beyond 2 m [M], and it says plane-wave reflectometry is unreliable from about 60 mm diameter on (x about 2660 mm). Values are kept as read: the spikes at 560 to 800 mm (change valves) and 1100 to
#    1300 mm (tuning slide and water keys) are not smoothed. Stations x = 20 to 160 mm are the reflectometer's red and green curves where the blue is hidden (they coincide on the shared path). The station at x = 0 is the red curve's first pixel,
#    7.66 mm at x = 6 mm, carried to x = 0. Data and method: descriptions/horn/kemp2008-fig8-9-bb-schmid-stations.tsv (the scripts of the run are not committed).
# 3. BELL END, 17 cones from the reflectometer's last diameter (98.75 mm, x = 2770 mm) to the rim: the OUTER profile of the bell of a Kruspe F/B-flat double horn (Metropolitan Museum object 503607, 1904-19, public domain), read from two photographs,
#    front and back, side-on [R]: silhouette by colour saturation, rim line = the Met's stated bell diameter 314.3 mm [M] as the scale (0.1545 mm per pixel), width along the rim direction at each distance z from the rim, mean of six extractions
#    (two views, three thresholds), range +-1.5 mm for z = 30 to 85 mm, +-3 mm at z = 20, +-7 mm at z = 10 mm. The Met's stated height 41.9 cm does NOT match the photograph at that scale (26 percent), so the scale rests on one number. The wall (about
#    0.3 to 0.5 mm) is not subtracted. The Schmid's own bell is not measured: this is ANOTHER HORN'S BELL joined at its outer diameter of 98.75 mm (z = 83.0 mm from its rim); rim diameter 314.3 mm (nominal Schmid and Alexander bells 310 mm [N]).
#    At z = 0, 5 and 15 mm the measuring line met a second run of the silhouette (the rolled rim, a neighbouring tube); those values are kept and are the least certain. Data and method:
#    descriptions/horn/met503607-kruspe-bell-stations.tsv (the scripts of the run are not committed). The join is a straight cone between the two sets of stations: no flare law is fitted.
# Total: 166 cones, 2.9107 m from the cup rim to the bell rim; the open Bb series of a Bb1 horn has a half wavelength near 2.9 m.
# NOT in this card: the valve loops and slides, the water keys as apertures, the hand in the bell (no geometry published; Watts 2009 and Dell et al. 2010 give impedance only), the receiver socket and the leadpipe's soldered joints.
#
# --- ambient and bore gas (borrowed, byte for byte from the live horn cards) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 293.15 K, dry; UNKNOWN: the temperature and humidity of the Iowa anechoic chamber. The bore holds the steady full-breath state of the clarinet and oboe cards (27 C, saturated, 4.5 % CO2, 16.5 % O2 dry basis: Coltman 1966 and
# Nederveen 1969 through Fletcher and Rossing 1998), uniform along the bore: a woodwind source borrowed for a brass bore, the end-member and not a played-in state (Boutin, Smith and Wolfe 2020, JASA 148:1817).
full_scale_pa 50
ambient moist_air 293.15 101325 0
bore_gas pack breath-woodwind-exhaled-coltman1966-nederveen1969
#
# --- lip (borrowed, byte for byte from the live horn cards) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO HORN-PLAYER LIP IS PUBLISHED. Cullen (2000), PhD thesis, University of Edinburgh, Table A.2 p.253 embouchure 19 (the tightest of 20): fL1 237.0 Hz, QL1 12.0, 1/muL1 0.072 m2/kg [M]; b 9 mm and H0 0.25 mm are ESTIMATED by the thesis;
# ARTIFICIAL latex lips on a trombone-size mouthpiece (outward-striking mode only), not a horn player's. The lip pre-stress of the schedule is the free player input: the setting of each held note is a ladder read from the render, never a pitch.
lip_response 237.0 12.0 0.072 0.009 0.00025 1.0e-4
lip_source Cullen (2000) PhD Edinburgh Table A.2 p.253 embouchure 19 (tightest of 20): fL1 237.0 Hz, QL1 12.0, 1/muL1 0.072 m2/kg, b 9 mm, H0 0.25 mm; artificial latex lips on a trombone-size mouthpiece, outward-striking mode only
# lip_area_exponent: appended per note by render.sh. MEASURED on a TROMBONIST, per note: Bromage, Campbell and Gilbert (2010), 'Open areas of vibrating lips in trombone playing' (HAL hal-00474644v1), Table 2, one player, mezzo forte:
# q = 1.2 at Bb1, 1.3 at Bb2, 1.6 at F3, 1.9 at F4; linear interpolation in MIDI between the printed notes, no extrapolation (a note above F4 carries no row). A trombonist's lips on a trombone mouthpiece, not a horn player's.
#
# --- mouthpiece cup volume, termination, modes, radiation load (borrowed, byte for byte) ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The cup is geometry in the bore above (rim radius 8.3 mm falling to a 2.25 mm throat), so the lumped cup volume is the smallest the format admits (1 mm^3): a format floor, not a measurement.
cup_volume_m3 1e-9
termination flanged
modes 3 1
modal_baffle 1e-5 33554432
# constriction_noise: the printed duct-constriction law of Oldham and Ukpoho (J. Sound Vib. 140(2), 1990) on the lip channel, an extrapolation from dampers and orifice plates in a 300 mm duct at 10-25 m/s; the radius is the cup rim radius of this
# card's first bore record (8.3 mm); the seed is a declared label.
constriction_noise 7 8.3e-3
# NUMERICAL CONTROL (not physics, not fitted to any recording), as on the live horn cards: the trombone card's mouth_flow_tail_tol 2e-6 is refused on a 4.5 m bore at the 65536-tap ceiling; the tolerance declared here is the live horn cards' 4e-6.
mouth_flow_tail_tol 4e-6
#
# --- bore: 166 cones, [M]/[R]/[B] as stated above, units metres: inlet radius, outlet radius, length -----------------------------------------------------------------
# mouthpiece cup (Paxman 3A, [M] ends, one cone [B])
cone 8.3000000e-03 2.2500000e-03 2.6000000e-02
# mouthpiece backbore (Paxman 3A throat to the reflectometer's first diameter, one cone [B])
cone 2.2500000e-03 3.8300000e-03 3.1700000e-02
# main bore, Schmid Bb side, acoustic pulse reflectometry [R]: stations at x = 0, 20, 40 ... 2770 mm
cone 3.8300000e-03 3.9850000e-03 2.0000000e-02
