bore_source Kemp, Chick, Campbell and Hendrie (2008) Acoustics'08 Paris Figs 8 and 9 'Bb Schmid' (Engelbert Schmid Bb/Eb-alto double horn, rotary valves, valves open), 166 cones through 148 stations read from the raster figures (read error 0.03 mm to 4 mm by diameter, source bound +-0.1 mm to +-0.8 mm); mouthpiece Paxman 3A of an Alexander double horn (Edinburgh MIMEd 16358: cup 16.6, throat 4.5, cup depth about 26, shank 31.7 mm); bell end read from photographs of a Kruspe F/B-flat double horn (Metropolitan Museum 503607, rim diameter 314.3 mm); population stand-in for the Iowa horn, whose model is unstated
#
# --- microphone and observer ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UIowa MIS pages: 'Distance 5 feet' = 1.524 m, anechoic chamber (as on the live horn cards). On the bell axis in the baffle frame; UNKNOWN: the player's bell-to-microphone orientation and the microphone's directivity are not modelled.
# The band is the resolved band of the 128-ring, 512-point receiver on the 0.157 m bell rim, declared at 2300 Hz.
microphone 0 0 1.524 128 512 2300
# observer_gas: the exterior gas's absorption and dispersion on the bell-to-microphone paths (classical Stokes-Kirchhoff law, the form the flute pages use); it changes observation only, not the lip, the bore or the bell load (borrowed, byte for byte).
observer_gas stokes-kirchhoff 4096 1000000 50000000
